$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>How a maintenance revision became a divorce roadmap</h2>
<p>The petitioners — the wife and the couple&rsquo;s minor son, represented by his mother — had moved the Family Court for enhanced maintenance (M.P. No. 1674 of 2023 in M.C. No. 448 of 2023), and carried the challenge to the High Court in revision. Before Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Shamim Ahmed, the parties reported that mediation had succeeded: the husband would pay Rs 65,00,000 as permanent alimony in full and final settlement, of which Rs 45,00,000 stood paid.</p>
<p>With the money substantially moved and a Section 13B petition on file, the real risk to the settlement was time — the drift of an overloaded Family Court list. The High Court addressed exactly that.</p>

<h2>The directions</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Expedition.</strong> The VII Additional Family Court, Chennai is to take up the Section 13B petition and proceed expeditiously, &ldquo;without granting unnecessary adjournments&rdquo;.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Waiver to be considered.</strong> Any application to waive the six-month period under Section 13B(2) is to be considered independently, applying Amardeep Singh v. Harveen Kaur, (2017) 8 SCC 746.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Payment milestone.</strong> The balance Rs 20,00,000 is payable to the wife when evidence is recorded before the Family Court — tying the final tranche to a verifiable procedural step.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Supervision.</strong> The Registry is to transmit the order, the Family Court is to proceed uninfluenced by the revision&rsquo;s pendency, and the matter is listed on 30 November 2026 for reporting compliance.</div>
</div>

<h2>Why milestone-linked settlements work</h2>
<p>The architecture here deserves attention from anyone drafting matrimonial settlements. Lump-sum promises fail in two directions: the paying spouse fears the divorce will stall after the money is gone, and the receiving spouse fears consent will be withdrawn after she gives up her claims. Linking tranches to procedural milestones — part on signing, part at first motion, the balance when evidence is recorded — keeps both sides invested in completing the journey. Adding a High Court compliance date, as this order does, supplies the supervision that makes the timetable real.</p>
<p>The waiver question completes the design. Under <em>Amardeep Singh</em>, the cooling-off period of Section 13B(2) is directory: where the marriage is dead, the separation long, and every dispute — alimony, custody, pending cases — genuinely settled, courts can waive the second six-month wait rather than prolong the parties&rsquo; agony. A mediated package of the present kind is precisely the situation the Supreme Court had in mind, though the decision on waiver remains the Family Court&rsquo;s to make on the material before it.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Put the full payment schedule, with milestones, into the mediation agreement itself — and record part-payments by instrument number.</p>
  <p>List every pending case between the parties in the settlement, with an agreed mechanism for withdrawal or quashing of each.</p>
  <p>Where expedition matters, ask the supervising court for a compliance date; a listed follow-up hearing concentrates minds wonderfully.</p>
  <p>File the waiver application under Section 13B(2) with evidence of the settlement and the length of separation, squarely addressing the Amardeep Singh conditions.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information. Settlement terms are case-specific and nothing here is a template for any other matter.</div>
